  <Patch Id="QQ26jKaFA1r4SWPs6EYAZi">
    <Canvas Id="AKGzKuh010ejqDzVgkFE4B" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="FyUWPxl3ZcUs9ev8CpoeWW">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="IuCfgbjki2WaJWGYFOnaLo">
        <Canvas Id="NKJt9ZpR4R3b4YM0hUFxuZ" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="Luq5eAe7nHeGKDeqBLIxe4" Bounds="-840,-120,660,820" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="EXPLANATION - You do not have to read everything&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The claim in the title, on one screen. The same 754 features - Haneda airport's runways, taxiways&#xD;&#xA;and buildings, the extract chapter 09 draws - live TWICE on this disk. ONE FILE is&#xD;&#xA;Assets\haneda.geojson: about 307 KB, everything, always. MANY FILES is Assets\haneda-tiles\:&#xD;&#xA;the identical features cut into an 8 x 8 grid, one small file per cell that has anything in it,&#xD;&#xA;median about 9 KB.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;POINT ANYWHERE IN THE GRID. Both sides answer the same question - how many features have their&#xD;&#xA;centre in this cell? - and their IN CELL numbers always agree. Now read the cost lines: the&#xD;&#xA;one-file side read all 307 KB and parsed all 754 features, and does it AGAIN for every cell you&#xD;&#xA;visit. The many-files side read one small file - about 26x less over the runways, hundreds of&#xD;&#xA;times less at the edges - because your cell's number COMPUTED THE FILENAME. Look at 'the file&#xD;&#xA;your cell chose': the question decided what to read before anything was read.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Point OUTSIDE the grid, or at an empty cell (half the 64 have nothing in them): the many-files&#xD;&#xA;side reads NOTHING - the file does not exist, and that absence is itself the answer, readable off&#xD;&#xA;the directory. The patch asks Exists BEFORE it reads, so a missing file is an answer here, never&#xD;&#xA;an error. The one-file side still pays full price to say 0.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHY IT MATTERS beyond one airport: on this disk both sides feel instant. Make the file remote and&#xD;&#xA;bytes become seconds and money - and this gap is the entire reason cloud-native formats exist:&#xD;&#xA;GeoParquet groups rows spatially, Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF tiles its raster internally, PMTiles&#xD;&#xA;puts a directory in front of a tile pyramid - all so a small question can compute WHERE to read&#xD;&#xA;instead of reading everything. This course's own map chapters have fetched 3,422 tiles, 73 MB,&#xD;&#xA;from pyramids that hold roughly 366 BILLION - the most-used source's share is 0.0000004%, and the&#xD;&#xA;maps always felt complete. Every map you have ever dragged works the way the right-hand rows do;&#xD;&#xA;this screen is that idea at a size you can see whole.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;HONEST EDGES, so this does not overclaim. The cut puts each feature in the ONE cell holding its&#xD;&#xA;centroid - real tilers CLIP features or DUPLICATE them across cells, and must say which. The&#xD;&#xA;centroid rule is NetTopologySuite's own, on purpose: the first cut used a simpler vertex average&#xD;&#xA;and two features near a cell edge changed sides - a partition is a claim about the data, and the&#xD;&#xA;claim must name its formula (the recorded cut: lab\cut-tiles.py). The one-file side re-reads on&#xD;&#xA;every cell change ON PURPOSE: that is what a fresh question against a remote file costs, and a&#xD;&#xA;cache here would hide the thing being measured. And the two 'read' rows count characters out of&#xD;&#xA;the reader, not bytes - for this data they differ by a tenth of a percent.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  copy Assets\haneda-tiles somewhere safe, then delete ONE tile file and point at its cell: the&#xD;&#xA;  many-files side now fails honestly (read 0) while the one-file side still answers. A partition&#xD;&#xA;  trades one big dependency for many small ones - you can now lose a PIECE of the world.&#xD;&#xA;  recut coarser or finer: lab\cut-tiles.py, change N. When does a tile stop being worth a file?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS for what you just did: the grid is a PARTITION (a tiling scheme); reading only the&#xD;&#xA;touched partition is SPATIAL SUBSETTING; the always-everything side is a FULL SCAN. Chapter 11&#xD;&#xA;pruned who gets ASKED (an index, inside memory); this screen prunes what gets READ (a layout, on&#xD;&#xA;disk). Same shape, one storey down.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 11              the same pruning inside memory - 100,000 points, one index&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 09              where files like haneda.geojson come from, and what they owe&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 14              this exact idea over the network: a remote file, read by address&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Assets: Assets\haneda.geojson and its 8x8 cut (OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL - see&#xD;&#xA;THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md). No network access: everything here reads from this disk.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
